761</td><td>155,657</td><td>77,608</td><td>8,294</td><td>7,684</td><td>9,465</td><td>6,754</td><td>-781</td><td>0.7952</td></tr></table>\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"table\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [137, 85, 188, 125], \"page_id\": 108}], \"content\": \"106\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [406, 207, 1229, 255], \"page_id\": 108}], \"content\": \"INTRODUCTION: Exchange Stabilization Fund\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"title\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [135, 264, 801, 478], \"page_id\": 108}], \"content\": \"To stabilize the exchange value of the dollar, the Exchange Stabilization Fund (ESF) was established pursuant to chapter 6, section 10 of the Gold Reserve Act of January 30, 1934 (codified at 31 United States Code 5302), which authorized establishment of a Treasury fund to be operated under the exclusive control of the Secretary, with approval of the President.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [135, 483, 801, 585], \"page_id\": 108}], \"content\": \"Subsequent amendment of the Gold Reserve Act modified the original purpose somewhat to reflect termination of the fixed exchange rate system.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [133, 585, 800, 783], \"page_id\": 108}], \"content\": \"Resources of the fund include dollar balances, partially invested in U.S. Government securities, special drawing rights (SDRs), and balances of foreign currencies. Principal sources of income (+) or loss (-) for the fund are profits (+) or losses (-) on SDRs and foreign exchange, as well as interest earned on assets.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [823, 269, 1492, 547], \"page_id\": 108}], \"content\": \"Table ESF-1 presents the assets, liabilities, and capital of the fund. The figures are in U.S. dollars or their equivalents based on current exchange rates computed according to the accrual method of accounting. The capital account represents the original capital appropriated to the fund by Congress of $2 billion, minus a subsequent transfer of $1.8 billion to pay for the initial U.S. quota subscription to the International Monetary Fund. Gains and losses are reflected in the cumulative net income (+) or loss (-) account.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [832, 556, 1499, 778], \"page_id\": 108}], \"content\": \"Table ESF-2 shows the results of operations by quarter. Figures are in U.S. dollars or their equivalents computed according to the accrual method. \\\"Profit (+) or loss (-) on foreign exchange\\\" includes realized profits or losses. \\\"Adjustment for change in valuation of SDR holdings and allocations\\\" reflects net gain or loss on revaluation of SDR holdings and allocations for the quarter.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [372, 839, 1259, 884], \"page_id\": 108}], \"content\": \"TABLE ESF-1.\\u2014Balances as of Sept. 30, 2003, and Dec. 31, 2003\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"caption\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [461, 896, 1175, 922], \"page_id\": 108}], \"content\": \"[In thousands of dollars. Source: Office of the Assistant Secretary of the Treasury for Management]\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"caption\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [133, 924, 1499, 1855], \"page_id\": 108}], \"content\": \"<table><tr><th>Assets, liabilities, and capital</th><th>Sept. 30, 2003</th><th>Oct. 1, 2003, through Dec. 31, 2003</th><th>Dec. 31, 2003</th></tr><tr><td><b>Assets</b></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>U.S. dollars:</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Held with Treasury:</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>U.S. Government securities</td><td>10,502,415</td><td>26,768</td><td>10,529,183</td></tr><tr><td>Special drawing rights 1</td><td>12,062,467</td><td>575,172</td><td>12,637,639</td></tr><tr><td>Foreign exchange and securities:</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>European euro</td><td>10,075,954</td><td>869,781</td><td>10,945,735</td></tr><tr><td>Japanese yen</td><td>8,475,538</td><td>340,494</td><td>8,816,032</td></tr><tr><td>Accounts receivable</td><td>102,567</td><td>22,871</td><td>125,438</td></tr><tr><td>Total assets</td><td>41,218,941</td><td>1,835,086</td><td>43,054,027</td></tr><tr><td><b>Liabilities and capital</b></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Current liabilities:</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Accounts payable</td><td>18,181</td><td>79,180</td><td>97,361</td></tr><tr><td>Total current liabilities</td><td>18,181</td><td>79,180</td><td>97,361</td></tr><tr><td>Other liabilities:</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>SDR certificates</td><td>2,200,000</td><td>-</td><td>2,200,000</td></tr><tr><td>SDR allocations</td><td>7,005,299</td><td>275,256</td><td>7,280,555</td></tr><tr><td>Total other liabilities</td><td>9,205,299</td><td>275,256</td><td>9,480,555</td></tr><tr><td>Capital:</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Capital account</td><td>200,000</td><td>-</td><td>200,000</td></tr><tr><td>Net income (+) or loss (-) (see table ESF -2)</td><td>31,795,461</td><td>1,480,650</td><td>33,276,111</td></tr><tr><td>Total capital</td><td>31,995,461</td><td>1,480,650</td><td>33,476,111</td></tr><tr><td>Total liabilities and capital</td><td>41,218,941</td><td>1,835,086</td><td>43,054,027</td></tr></table>\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"table\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [140, 1874, 397, 1902], \"page_id\": 108}], \"content\": \"See footnote on the following page.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [594, 101, 1044, 123], \"page_id\": 109}], \"content\": \"EXCHANGE STABILIZATION FUND\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1444, 101, 1492, 123], \"page_id\": 109}], \"content\": \"107\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [552, 196, 1086, 229], \"page_id\": 109}], \"content\": \"TABLE ESF-2.\\u2014Income and Expense\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [464, 245, 1169, 264], \"page_id\": 109}], \"content\": \"[In thousands of dollars. Source: Office of the Assistant Secretary of the Treasury for Management]\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [142, 266, 1492, 1223], \"page_id\": 109}], \"content\": \"<table><tr><th></th><th>Current quarter<br>Oct. 1, 2003,<br>through<br>Dec. 31, 2003</th><th>Fiscal year to date<br>Oct. 1, 2003,<br>through<br>Dec. 31, 2003</th></tr><tr><td><b>Income and expense</b></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Profit (+) or loss (-) on:</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Foreign exchange</td><td>1,167,806</td><td>1,167,806</td></tr><tr><td>Adjustment for change in valuation of SDR holdings and allocations <sup>1</sup></td><td>202,771</td><td>202,771</td></tr><tr><td>Interest (+) or net charges (-) on:</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>SDRs</td><td>20,500</td><td>20,500</td></tr><tr><td>U.S. Government securities</td><td>26,768</td><td>26,768</td></tr><tr><td>Foreign exchange</td><td>62,805</td><td>62,805</td></tr><tr><td>Commissions</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>Income from operations</td><td>1,480,650</td><td>1,480,650</td></tr><tr><td>Net income (+) or loss (-)</td><td>1,480,650</td><td>1,480,650</td></tr></table>\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"table\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [160, 1247, 809, 1318], \"page_id\": 109}], \"content\": \"Beginning July 1974, the International Monetary Fund adopted a technique for valuing the SDRs based on a weighted average of exchange rates for the currencies of selected member countries. The U.S. SDR holdings and allocations are valued on this basis beginning July 1974.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [843, 1247, 1472, 1358], \"page_id\": 109}], \"content\": \"Note.\\u2014 Annual balance sheets for fiscal years 1934 through 1940 appeared in the 1940 \\\"Annual Report of the Secretary of the Treasury\\\" and those for succeeding years appeared in subsequent reports through 1980. Quarterly balance sheets beginning with December 31, 1938, have been published in the \\\"Treasury Bulletin.\\\" Data from inception to September 30, 1978, may be found on the statements published in the January 1979 \\\"Treasury Bulletin.\\\"\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1062, 186, 1486, 401], \"page_id\": 110}], \"content\": null, \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"figure\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1158, 420, 1486, 556], \"page_id\": 110}], \"content\": \"Financial Report of the United States Government Excerpt\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1341, 599, 1481, 641], \"page_id\": 110}], \"content\": \"TrustFunds\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1443, 92, 1492, 132], \"page_id\": 111}], \"content\": \"111\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [355, 97, 1284, 127], \"page_id\": 111}], \"content\": \"FINANCIAL REPORT OF THE UNITED STATES GOVERNMENT, EXCERPT\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [209, 205, 1421, 262], \"page_id\": 111}], \"content\": \"INTRODUCTION: Financial Report of the United States Government\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [137, 290, 805, 478], \"page_id\": 111}], \"content\": \"The following pages are excerpted from the 2003 Financial Report of the United States Government (Financial Report). The report and accompanying information, prepared and published annually by the Financial Management Service, are modeled after corporate-type reports and are developed on an accrual basis of accounting.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [834, 290, 1495, 448], \"page_id\": 111}], \"content\": \"accounting systems Governmentwide. Treasury also presents a management discussion and analysis, the customary notes to the financial statements, a section on stewardship information, and supplemental information in the complete document.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [829, 446, 1481, 573], \"page_id\": 111}], \"content\": \"The General Accounting Office (GAO) is required by law to audit the Financial Report. GAO's report, as well as the complete Financial Report, can be accessed easily through the internet at www.fms.treas.gov/fr.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [137, 474, 803, 570], \"page_id\": 111}], \"content\": \"The excerpts represent some of the most noteworthy information contained in the Financial Report. Data for the Financial Report are compiled from Federal program agency\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [142, 99, 188, 123], \"page_id\": 112}], \"content\": \"112\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [355, 97, 1279, 125], \"page_id\": 112}], \"content\": \"FINANCIAL REPORT OF THE UNITED STATES GOVERNMENT, EXCERPT\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [142, 196, 1362, 471], \"page_id\": 112}], \"content\": \"Financial Statements of the United States Government for the Years Ended September 30, 2003 and September 30, 2002\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [153, 554, 619, 594], \"page_id\": 112}], \"content\": \"Statements of Net Cost\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [144, 651, 1479, 893], \"page_id\": 112}], \"content\": \"These statements present the net cost of fiscal years 2003 and 2002 Government operations. For the purposes of this document, \\\"Government\\\" refers to the United States Government. It categorizes costs by Chief Financial Officer Act entities and other significant entities. Costs are presented by department on an accrual basis, while the budget presents costs by obligations and outlays based on functions on a cash basis. In the Statements of Net Cost, the costs are divided between the corresponding departments and entities mentioned above, providing greater accountability by showing the relationship of the agencies' net cost to the Governmentwide net cost. The focus of the budget of the United States is by agency. Budgets are prepared, defended, and monitored by agency. In reporting by agency, we are assisting the external users in assessing the budget integrity, operating performance, stewardship, and systems and control of the Federal Government.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [200, 896, 825, 922], \"page_id\": 112}], \"content\": \"These statements contain the following three components:\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [200, 929, 1452, 992], \"page_id\": 112}], \"content\": \"Gross cost\\u2014This is the full cost of all the departments and entities. These costs are assigned on a cause-and-effect basis, or reasonably allocated to the corresponding departments and entities.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [200, 995, 1370, 1023], \"page_id\": 112}], \"content\": \"Earned revenue\\u2014This is revenue the Government earned by providing goods and services to the public at a price.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [200, 1023, 1022, 1054], \"page_id\": 112}], \"content\": \"Net cost\\u2014This is computed by subtracting earned revenue from gross cost.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [144, 1056, 1481, 1358], \"page_id\": 112}], \"content\": \"Because of their specific functions, most of the costs originally associated with the General Services Administration (GSA) and the Office of Personnel Management (OPM) have been allocated to and reflected in the costs of their user agencies. The remaining costs for GSA and OPM on the Statements of Net Cost are the administrative operating costs, the expenses from prior and past costs from health and pension plan amendments, and the actuarial gains and losses for these agencies. $123.4 billion has been allocated out of OPM to the agencies. This represents health and pension benefits that are not reported in the individual agency statements. The interest on Treasury securities held by the public is part of the Department of the Treasury's (Treasury) responsibilities, but because of its importance, and the dollar amounts, it is reported separately in these statements. Net cost for Governmentwide reporting purposes includes GSA and OPM agency allocations, and is net of intragovernmental eliminations. For this reason, individual agency net cost amounts will not agree with the agency's financial statements.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [144, 1360, 1412, 1511], \"page_id\": 112}], \"content\": \"The Department of Homeland Security (DHS) Reorganization Plan was enacted on November 25, 2002, and beginning with this 2003 Financial Report of the United States Government (Financial Report), DHS is reported as a separate entity. Refer to Note 1A\\u2014Reporting Entity for further information. Also, refer to the Management\\u2019s Discussion and Analysis section for information concerning DHS and the mission and organization of the components of the Government of the United States.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [153, 1568, 1260, 1608], \"page_id\": 112}], \"content\": \"Statements of Operations and Changes in Net Position\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 11, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [144, 1664, 1481, 1780], \"page_id\": 112}], \"content\": \"These statements report the results of Government operations. They include unearned revenues that are generated principally by the Government's sovereign power to tax, levy duties, and assess fines and penalties. These statements also cover the cost of Government operations, net of revenue earned from the sale of goods and services to the public (earned revenues). They further include any adjustments and unreconciled transactions that affect the net position.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 12, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [353, 97, 1286, 127], \"page_id\": 113}], \"content\": \"FINANCIAL REPORT OF THE UNITED STATES GOVERNMENT, EXCERPT\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1441, 97, 1488, 127], \"page_id\": 113}], \"content\": \"113\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [149, 214, 335, 262], \"page_id\": 113}], \"content\": \"Revenue\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 295, 1459, 358], \"page_id\": 113}], \"content\": \"Individual income tax and tax withholdings consist of Federal Insurance Contributions Act (FICA)/Self-Employment Contributions Act (SECA) taxes and other taxes including payroll taxes collected from other agencies.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 358, 1459, 417], \"page_id\": 113}], \"content\": \"Excise taxes consist of taxes collected for various items, such as airline tickets, gasoline products, distilled spirits and imported liquor, tobacco, firearms, and others.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 417, 1404, 575], \"page_id\": 113}], \"content\": \"Miscellaneous earned revenues consist of earned revenues received from the public with virtually no associated cost. This category includes revenues generated by the Federal Communications Commission from the sale of spectrum licenses to promote open-air communication services to the public (spectrum auctions). It also includes rents and royalties on the Outer Continental Shelf Lands resulting from the leasing and development of mineral resources on public lands.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [149, 625, 883, 674], \"page_id\": 113}], \"content\": \"Net Cost of Government Operations\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 705, 1472, 768], \"page_id\": 113}], \"content\": \"The net cost of Government operations (which is gross cost less earned revenue) flows through from the Statements of Net Cost.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [149, 818, 1144, 912], \"page_id\": 113}], \"content\": \"Unreconciled Transactions Affecting the Change in Net Position\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 948, 1468, 1042], \"page_id\": 113}], \"content\": \"Unreconciled transactions are adjustments needed to bring the change in net position into balance due to unreconciled and unaccounted for differences in the consolidated financial statements. Refer to Note 16\\u2014Unreconciled Transactions Affecting the Change in Net Position for detailed information.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [149, 1094, 832, 1146], \"page_id\": 113}], \"content\": \"Net Position, Beginning of Period\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 1176, 1483, 1238], \"page_id\": 113}], \"content\": \"The net position, beginning of period reflects the net position reported on the prior year's balance sheet as of the end of that fiscal year.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 11, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [149, 1289, 670, 1339], \"page_id\": 113}], \"content\": \"Prior Period Adjustments\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 12, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 1372, 1484, 1433], \"page_id\": 113}], \"content\": \"Prior period adjustments are revisions to adjust the beginning net position. Refer to Note 1B\\u2014Basis of Accounting and Revenue Recognition, and Note 17\\u2014Change in Accounting Principle and Prior Period Adjustments for detailed information.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 13, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [149, 1483, 703, 1532], \"page_id\": 113}], \"content\": \"Net Position, End of Period\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 14, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [175, 1568, 1209, 1598], \"page_id\": 113}], \"content\": \"The net position, end of period amount reflects the net position as of the end of the fiscal year.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 15, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [149, 1648, 1328, 1744], \"page_id\": 113}], \"content\": \"Reconciliations of Net Operating Cost and Unified Budget Deficit\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 16, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 1794, 1483, 1855], \"page_id\": 113}], \"content\": \"The purpose of the reconciliation is to report how the proprietary net operating cost and the unified budget deficit relate to each other. The premise of the reconciliation is that the accrual and budgetary accounting basis share transaction data.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 17, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 1855, 1386, 1919], \"page_id\": 113}], \"content\": \"These statements report the reconciliation of the results of operations (net operating cost) on the Statements of Operations and Changes in Net Position to the unified budget deficit in the President\\u2019s budget.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 18, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [144, 94, 195, 123], \"page_id\": 114}], \"content\": \"114\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [355, 97, 1284, 127], \"page_id\": 114}], \"content\": \"FINANCIAL REPORT OF THE UNITED STATES GOVERNMENT, EXCERPT\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [142, 191, 1475, 377], \"page_id\": 114}], \"content\": \"Receipts and outlays in the President's budget are measured primarily on a cash basis and differ from the basis of accounting measures used in the Financial Report. These statements begin with the net results of operations (net operating cost), where operating revenues are reported on a modified cash basis of accounting and the net cost of Government operations on an accrual basis of accounting. Reconciling items to (1) operating revenues include net accrual related to taxes receivables and (2) net cost of Government operations include items such as changes in liabilities for military, veteran and civilian benefits, as well as depreciation expenses on fixed assets and changes in environmental liabilities.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [151, 431, 1175, 528], \"page_id\": 114}], \"content\": \"Components of Net Operating Cost Not Part of the Budget Deficit\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"title\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 561, 1474, 632], \"page_id\": 114}], \"content\": \"This information includes the operating components, such as the changes of benefits payable for veterans, military and civilian employees, and the environmental liabilities and depreciation expense not included in the budget results.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [151, 674, 1155, 776], \"page_id\": 114}], \"content\": \"Components of the Budget Deficit Not Part of Net Operating Cost\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"title\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [140, 808, 1477, 900], \"page_id\": 114}], \"content\": \"This information includes the budget components, such as capitalized fixed assets, changes in accounts receivable, and increases in other assets not included in the operating results. These items are typically part of the balance sheets only, and are not part of the operating results.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [149, 952, 1235, 1049], \"page_id\": 114}], \"content\": \"Statements of Changes in Cash Balance from Unified Budget and Other Activities\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"title\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [137, 1096, 1475, 1193], \"page_id\": 114}], \"content\": \"The primary purpose of these statements is to report how the annual unified budget deficit relates to the change in the Government's operating cash balance and debt held by the public. It explains why the unified budget deficit normally would not result in an equivalent change in the Government's operating cash balance.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [135, 1193, 1479, 1280], \"page_id\": 114}], \"content\": \"These statements reconcile the unified budget deficit to the change in operating cash during the fiscal year, and explain how the budget deficits (fiscal years 2003 and 2002) are financed. A budget deficit is the result of expenditures exceeding receipts (revenue) during a particular fiscal year.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [135, 1280, 1490, 1433], \"page_id\": 114}], \"content\": \"In depicting how the unified budget deficits were financed, these statements show that in fiscal years 2003 and 2002, the greatest amounts were net new borrowings from the public. Other transactions also required cash disbursements and are not part of the repayments of the debt. These other transactions, such as the issuance of student loans or premiums on early buyback of public debt, required cash payments and contributed to the use of the surplus or deficit. These statements show the differences between accrual and cash budgetary basis, mainly because of timing differences in the financial statements.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [153, 1487, 472, 1527], \"page_id\": 114}], \"content\": \"Balance Sheets\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 11, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [140, 1584, 1459, 1678], \"page_id\": 114}], \"content\": \"The balance sheets show the Government's assets and liabilities. When combined with stewardship information, this information presents a more comprehensive understanding of the Government's financial position. All of the line items on the balance sheets are described in the Notes to the Financial Statements.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 12, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [153, 1730, 295, 1770], \"page_id\": 114}], \"content\": \"Assets\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 13, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [138, 1806, 1470, 1907], \"page_id\": 114}], \"content\": \"Assets included on the balance sheets are resources of the Government that remain available to meet future needs. The most significant assets that are reported on the balance sheets are property, plant, and equipment, inventories, and loans receivable. There are, however, other significant resources available to the Government that extend beyond the assets\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 14, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [355, 97, 1275, 127], \"page_id\": 115}], \"content\": \"FINANCIAL REPORT OF THE UNITED STATES GOVERNMENT, EXCERPT\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1444, 97, 1486, 125], \"page_id\": 115}], \"content\": \"115\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [142, 193, 1424, 285], \"page_id\": 115}], \"content\": \"presented in these financial statements. Those resources include stewardship assets, including natural resources (see Stewardship Information section), and the Government's sovereign powers to tax, regulate commerce, and set monetary policy.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [142, 288, 1463, 405], \"page_id\": 115}], \"content\": \"In May 2003, SFFAS No. 23, Eliminating the Category National Defense Property, Plant, and Equipment, removed national defense assets from Stewardship Information and included it on the balance sheet as part of inventories and related property, net and property, plant, and equipment, net. Refer to Note 1B\\u2014Basis of Accounting and Revenue Recognition; Note 6\\u2014Inventories and Related Property, Net; and Note 7\\u2014Property, Plant, and Equipment, Net for further information.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [144, 408, 1459, 469], \"page_id\": 115}], \"content\": \"Selected assets are highlighted in the Stewardship Information section of this report to demonstrate the Government's accountability for these assets. Stewardship assets include stewardship land and heritage assets.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [153, 526, 698, 566], \"page_id\": 115}], \"content\": \"Liabilities and Net Position\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [144, 603, 1483, 726], \"page_id\": 115}], \"content\": \"Liabilities are obligations of the Government resulting from prior actions that will require financial resources. The most significant liabilities reported on the balance sheets are Federal debt securities held by the public and accrued interest and Federal employee and veteran benefits payable. Liabilities also include social insurance benefits due and payable as of the reporting date.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [142, 728, 1479, 849], \"page_id\": 115}], \"content\": \"As with reported assets, the Government's responsibilities, policy commitments, and contingencies are much broader than these reported balance sheet liabilities. They include the social insurance programs disclosed in the Statements of Social Insurance in the Stewardship Information section, and a wide range of other programs under which the Government provides benefits and services to the people of this Nation, and certain future loss contingencies.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [142, 851, 1486, 1002], \"page_id\": 115}], \"content\": \"The magnitude and complexity of social insurance programs, coupled with the extreme sensitivity of projections relating to the many assumptions of the programs, produce a wide range of possible results. The Stewardship Responsibilities section describes the social insurance programs, reports long-range estimates that can be used to assess the financial condition of the programs, and explains some of the factors that impact the various programs. Using this information, readers can apply their own judgment as to the condition and sustainability of the individual programs.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [142, 1006, 1477, 1186], \"page_id\": 115}], \"content\": \"Each of the social insurance programs has an associated trust fund to account for its activity. The taxes collected for specific use are credited to the corresponding trust fund that will use these funds to meet a particular Government purpose. If the collections from taxes and other sources exceed the payments to the beneficiaries, the excess collections are invested in Treasury securities or \\u201cloaned\\u201d to the Treasury\\u2019s general fund; therefore, the trust fund balances do not represent cash. An explanation of the trust funds for social insurance and many of the other large trust funds is included in Note 20\\u2014Dedicated Collections. That note also contains information about trust fund receipts, disbursements, and assets.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [142, 1186, 1481, 1339], \"page_id\": 115}], \"content\": \"The Government has entered into contractual commitments requiring the future use of financial resources and has unresolved contingencies where existing conditions, situations, or circumstances create uncertainty about future losses. Commitments as well as contingencies that do not meet the criteria for recognition as liabilities on the balance sheets, but for which there is at least a reasonable possibility that losses have been incurred, are disclosed in Note 18\\u2014Contingent Liabilities and Note 19\\u2014Commitments.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [142, 1341, 1463, 1459], \"page_id\": 115}], \"content\": \"Because of its sovereign power to tax and borrow, and the country's wide economic base, the Government has unique access to financial resources through generating tax revenues and issuing Federal debt securities. This provides the Government with the ability to meet present obligations and those that are anticipated from future operations and are not reflected in net position.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 11, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [148, 99, 189, 123], \"page_id\": 116}], \"content\": \"116\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [355, 97, 1284, 125], \"page_id\": 116}], \"content\": \"FINANCIAL REPORT OF THE UNITED STATES GOVERNMENT, EXCERPT\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [182, 217, 1182, 325], \"page_id\": 116}], \"content\": \"Unit