yprus, Malta, and Slovenia. For data as of January 2009, also includes Slovakia.\\n5 Includes European, Latin American, Caribbean, Asian, African, and Middle East regional organizations.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1386, 1989, 1494, 2018], \"page_id\": 63}], \"content\": \"June 2009\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"page_footer\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [137, 92, 178, 132], \"page_id\": 64}], \"content\": \"58\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [663, 92, 965, 130], \"page_id\": 64}], \"content\": \"CAPITAL MOVEMENTS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [328, 238, 1304, 288], \"page_id\": 64}], \"content\": \"CHART CM-A.\\u2014U.S. Liabilities to Foreigners\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"title\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [155, 290, 1472, 332], \"page_id\": 64}], \"content\": \"Reported by U.S. Banks, Brokers, and Dealers with Respect to Selected Countries\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [230, 368, 1131, 1169], \"page_id\": 64}], \"content\": null, \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"figure\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [219, 1195, 1135, 1296], \"page_id\": 64}], \"content\": \"<table><tr><td><input type=\\\"checkbox\\\" checked> United Kingdom</td><td><input type=\\\"checkbox\\\"> All other Europe</td><td><input type=\\\"checkbox\\\" checked> Caribbean banking centers</td></tr><tr><td><input type=\\\"checkbox\\\"> Japan</td><td><input type=\\\"checkbox\\\" checked> All other Asia</td><td><input type=\\\"checkbox\\\"> All other countries</td></tr></table>\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"table\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1191, 394, 1521, 1190], \"page_id\": 64}], \"content\": \"U.S. liabilities to foreigners reported by U.S. banks were recorded at $4.5 trillion in March 2009, a decrease of $101 billion from yearend 2008. U.S. banking liabilities include foreign holdings of U.S. short-term securities but exclude foreign holdings of U.S. long-term securities. U.S. banking liabilities decreased about $91 billion in 2008, but increased about $696 billion in 2007 and about $824 billion in 2006. However, much of the increase in liabilities to foreigners in 2006 reflects changes in coverage in the bank-reported data in the first and second quarters of 2006. Between June and December of 2006, when data were reported on a consistent basis, banking liabilities increased about $250 billion.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [228, 1318, 1138, 1775], \"page_id\": 64}], \"content\": \"<table><tr><th colspan=\\\"6\\\">[In millions of dollars. Source: Treasury International Capital Reporting System]</th></tr><tr><th rowspan=\\\"2\\\">Country</th><th colspan=\\\"5\\\">Calendar years</th></tr><tr><th>2005</th><th>2006</th><th>2007</th><th>2008</th><th>Mar. 2009</th></tr><tr><td>United Kingdom</td><td>590,505</td><td>926,069</td><td>1,051,764</td><td>668,658</td><td>680,499</td></tr><tr><td>All other Europe</td><td>691,138</td><td>648,551</td><td>828,379</td><td>808,962</td><td>775,711</td></tr><tr><td>Caribbean banking centers 1</td><td>1,200,444</td><td>1,666,987</td><td>1,877,001</td><td>1,897,683</td><td>1,841,099</td></tr><tr><td>Japan</td><td>161,951</td><td>141,655</td><td>190,122</td><td>240,313</td><td>216,635</td></tr><tr><td>All other Asia</td><td>256,934</td><td>299,495</td><td>385,448</td><td>596,585</td><td>635,855</td></tr><tr><td>Subtotal</td><td>2,900,972</td><td>3,682,757</td><td>4,332,714</td><td>4,212,201</td><td>4,149,799</td></tr><tr><td>All other countries</td><td>285,773</td><td>327,602</td><td>373,596</td><td>402,809</td><td>364,565</td></tr><tr><td>Grand total</td><td>3,186,745</td><td>4,010,359</td><td>4,706,310</td><td>4,615,010</td><td>4,514,364</td></tr></table>\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"table\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [228, 1789, 1138, 1879], \"page_id\": 64}], \"content\": \"1 Includes Bahamas, Bermuda, Cayman Islands, Netherlands Antilles, and Panama. For data beginning June 2006, also includes the British Virgin Islands.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1184, 1230, 1525, 1879], \"page_id\": 64}], \"content\": \"U.S. banking liabilities are concentrated in international financial centers. The data on this page show that almost 60 percent of U.S. banking liabilities is currently recorded against the United Kingdom and banking centers in the Caribbean. These financial centers have recorded most of the growth in banking liabilities in recent years up through 2007. Since 2007, however, U.S. banking liabilities recorded against the United Kingdom have declined sharply. Meanwhile, banking liabilities to Asia have continued to grow at a rapid pace.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 9, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [140, 1987, 251, 2015], \"page_id\": 64}], \"content\": \"June 2009\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 10, \"type\": \"page_footer\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [667, 94, 969, 132], \"page_id\": 65}], \"content\": \"CAPITAL MOVEMENTS\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [1455, 94, 1494, 132], \"page_id\": 65}], \"content\": \"59\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"page_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [199, 191, 1433, 238], \"page_id\": 65}], \"content\": \"SECTION II.\\u2014Claims on Foreigners Reported by Banks in the United States\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [530, 250, 1109, 292], \"page_id\": 65}], \"content\": \"TABLE CM-II-1.\\u2014Total Claims by Type\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"section_header\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [446, 299, 1188, 328], \"page_id\": 65}], \"content\": \"[Position at end of period in mill